course: dictionary
lesson: 10937
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. To find the right one, read the context: the words and sentences around the word. «A vulture lands last, bald and scruffy, but it is one of nature's cleaners.» Bald, scruffy and cleaners show that a vulture is a bird here.
explain: The second meaning is informal, so it is used in everyday talk. «Circling like vultures» describes people crowding round to grab something. Ask yourself: is this a bird, or a person? The clues in the sentence tell you which.
explain: Writers also use animal words to paint a picture. The writer calls a vulture «a flying clean-up crew» because it cleans up dead animals. When you meet a comparison like this, ask why the writer chose that word.
fact: A vulture is a large bird with a bald or nearly bald head that eats the meat of dead animals.
fact: Vultures are scavengers: they clean up dead animals, which stops diseases spreading.
fact: Vultures soar high on warm air currents, and they find food by sight or smell.
fact: «Circling like vultures» means people crowding round to grab something.
fact: A «culture vulture» is a joking name for someone who loves the arts.
exercise: «A vulture lands last, bald and scruffy, but it is one of nature's cleaners.» What is a vulture in this sentence? | A person who waits for other people's bad luck | A kind of cloud | *A large bird that eats dead animals | A cleaning tool
exercise: «The reporters circled like vultures around the famous singer.» What does circled like vultures suggest? | Flying high on warm air | People who love art | *People crowding round to grab something | Sleeping peacefully
exercise: Why are vultures important in food chains? | They keep the sea full of fish | They only eat plants | They are the fastest birds in the sky | *They stop diseases spreading by cleaning up dead animals
test: Why are many vulture species in danger? | Their food is too sweet | *Poison harms them | They have grown too big to fly | They have been banned from the sky
test: A «culture vulture» is a joking name for someone who does what? | Waits for bad luck | Eats dead animals | Soars on warm air currents | *Loves the arts
exam: Why did the writer say «Vultures are not villains»? | To say vultures can fly high | *To say vultures are not wicked, because they help | To say vultures have bare heads | To say vultures are rare
